findings.body:
**Read each top-level packet value once before validating it.**

`packet-complete` validates `packet.after`, but `parsePatchShape` and later checks read `packet.after` again at `src/ops/review/internal/addressVerification.ts:448`, `:500`, `:529`, and `:558`. `packet.before`, `packet.patch`, `packet.finding.text`, and `packet.metadata.revision` are also read more than once. The dependency records are captured once, but these top-level fields are not.

If `after` is accessor-backed, the value validated at `:448` can differ from the value checked at `:500`. The validated snapshot can contain the quoted trigger while the later read does not. If the patch removes and re-adds the line that contains the trigger, the removed-line check at `:558` still matches. With `relevantDependenciesComplete` set to `true`, the verdict can then be `resolved`.

This path requires a caller to pass accessor-backed top-level fields. The inspected code states the data-only contract only for the dependency records. Capture each top-level value once, validate those captured values, and use them in every check, including the revision identity.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```diff
   const dependencies: CitedDependency[] = [];
+  // Read each top-level value once. Validation and every later check use these.
+  const patch = packet.patch;
+  const before = packet.before;
+  const after = packet.after;
```

```diff
-    const shape = parsePatchShape(packet.patch, packet.before, packet.after);
+    const shape = parsePatchShape(patch, before, after);
```

</details>

Apply the same substitution to the other `packet.before` and `packet.after` reads in `packet-complete`, `trigger-removed`, and `dependencies-covered`. Capture `packet.finding`, `packet.task`, and `packet.metadata.revision` the same way.
